course: dictionary
lesson: 6865
title: Too much of a good thing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Overdo means to do something too much. You can overdo the salt on your chips, or overdo exercise until you are worn out. The clue is the "over" at the front, which means more than is right or good.
explain: Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «Don't overdo the salt.» The salt is already too much, so the sentence warns you to use less. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Don't do the salt too much" makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: «Don't overdo it» is a common phrase, especially in sport and health lessons. It tells you to rest and not push your body too hard. In cooking, overdone food has been cooked too long, so it is dry or burnt.
explain: «Anthropomorphism means giving human feelings to animals or things, especially overdoing it.» Here overdoing it means giving human feelings to animals or things more than is sensible. The words around it show that overdo means going past the right amount.
fact: Overdo means to do something too much, or to use too much of something.
fact: «Don't overdo it» means rest and do not push your body too hard.
fact: Overdone food has been cooked too long, so it is dry or burnt.
fact: The verb changes its form: overdo, overdoes, overdid, overdone, overdoing.
fact: Teachers warn you not to overdo exclamation marks or describing words in your writing.
exercise: «Don't overdo the salt.» What does overdo mean here? | Throw the salt away | Use just the right amount | *Use too much | Add it at the end
exercise: Coach says, «Don't overdo it on the track today.» What is she warning you about? | Running too slowly | *Pushing too hard and getting too tired | Forgetting your trainers | Stopping for a drink
exercise: Which word describes food that has been cooked too long, so it is dry or burnt? | *overdone | soggy | hungry | overdo
test: Which is the correct past tense of overdo? | *overdid | overdo | overdoes | overdoing
test: Ben used five exclamation marks in one sentence. What might his teacher say? | *You have overdone the exclamation marks | You need more full stops | Your marks are too low | Well done, you have underdone them
exam: Which sentence uses overdone correctly? | The sausages were overdid | The sausages were overdoing in the pan | *The sausages were overdone and dry | The sausages overdone the pan
